# The ledger of `json/schema`: what the JSON Schema validators of the three
# languages let a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel
# answers. None of the three standard libraries has one, so the yardstick is
# the author's of 2026-11-17: Python's `jsonschema` 4.26.0 (with the
# `referencing` 0.37.0 it stands on), Go's
# `github.com/santhosh-tekuri/jsonschema/v6` v6.0.3 and Rust's `jsonschema`
# 0.61.2. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-17: `dir()` of the Python
# modules installed in the scratch folder, `go doc -all` of the Go module
# fetched there, and the item list docs.rs prints for the crate. The tests are
# the JSON Schema Test Suite (github.com/json-schema-org/JSON-Schema-Test-Suite,
# commit e2a68a15 of 2026-11-16), every case its `compatibility` gives to
# draft 7 or to 2020-12, run under each.

capability schema/compile  a schema read once, its references resolved, and used for many instances
  heroes  json/schema.compile, json/schema.compile_with, json/schema.Schema, json/schema.Options, json/schema.default_options
  go      Compiler, NewCompiler, Compiler.Compile, Compiler.MustCompile, Schema, Compiler.DefaultDraft
  rust    validator_for, options, options_for, ValidationOptions, ValidationOptions::build, Validator, draft7::new, draft7::options, draft202012::new, draft202012::options, attr validator
  python  Draft7Validator, Draft202012Validator, validators.validator_for, Validator.schema, Validator.evolve
  note    every reference is resolved and every keyword held to its form when the schema is compiled, as Go and Rust do, where Python finds a wrong reference only when an instance reaches it (observed: a `$ref` to `#/nothing` under a member no instance holds compiles and validates `{}`); a schema with no `$id` stands at `json-schema:///`, Rust's default base

capability schema/is-valid  whether an instance is valid against a schema
  heroes  json/schema.is_valid
  go      Schema.Validate (nil)
  rust    is_valid, Validator::is_valid, draft7::is_valid, draft202012::is_valid
  python  Validator.is_valid
  note    -

capability schema/validate  an instance validated, failing with what is wrong and where
  heroes  json/schema.validate
  go      Schema.Validate, ValidationError.Error, ValidationError.GoString, ValidationError.LocalizedError, ValidationError.LocalizedGoString
  rust    validate, Validator::validate, draft7::validate, draft202012::validate
  python  validate, Validator.validate, ValidationError, ValidationError.message
  note    fails `invalid` with the first error written by `format_error` and how many there are, Python's `validate` raising its best match

capability schema/errors  every error of an instance, each with its place in the instance and in the schema
  heroes  json/schema.errors_of, json/schema.Error
  go      ValidationError, ValidationError.InstanceLocation, ValidationError.SchemaURL, ValidationError.ErrorKind, ValidationError.Causes, ErrorKind, SchemaPath, SchemaPath.String, Position, Prop, Item, AllProp, AllItem, Types, Types.Add, Types.IsEmpty, Types.String, Types.ToStrings, LocalizableError
  rust    Validator::iter_errors, ValidationError, ValidationErrors, ErrorIterator, ValidationErrorKind, ValidationErrorParts, MaskedValidationError, error::TypeKind, paths::Location, paths::LocationSegment, paths::LazyLocation, JsonType, JsonTypeSet, JsonTypeSetIterator, types::JsonType, types::JsonTypeSet, types::JsonTypeSetIterator
  python  Validator.iter_errors, ValidationError.path, ValidationError.absolute_path, ValidationError.relative_path, ValidationError.schema_path, ValidationError.absolute_schema_path, ValidationError.relative_schema_path, ValidationError.json_path, ValidationError.validator, ValidationError.validator_value, ValidationError.instance, ValidationError.schema, ValidationError.context, ValidationError.cause, ValidationError.parent, ValidationError.create_from, exceptions.ErrorTree
  note    each error has its instance location and its keyword location as JSON pointers and its absolute keyword location, the three of 2020-12's output; an `anyOf` or a `oneOf` that fails gives its own error then its branches', where Python nests them in `context` and Go in `Causes`; Python's `ErrorTree`, errors by instance location, is a grouping of this list by `instance_location`. The kind of an error is its `keyword`, where Go and Rust give a type of each

capability schema/best-match  the one error most worth telling a person, of all an instance has
  heroes  json/schema.best_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  exceptions.best_match, exceptions.by_relevance, exceptions.relevance, exceptions.WEAK_MATCHES, exceptions.STRONG_MATCHES
  note    the error nearest the instance's root, of those the first that is no `anyOf`, `oneOf` or `not` summary; Python ranks by its own weak and strong keywords and answers the `anyOf`'s own error where none of its branches passes (observed on `{"anyOf": [{"type": "string"}, {"minimum": 5}]}` and 1), where this answers the first branch's

capability schema/output  the standard output of JSON Schema: the flag and the basic list
  heroes  json/schema.to_output, json/schema.is_valid
  go      ValidationError.FlagOutput, ValidationError.BasicOutput, FlagOutput, OutputUnit, OutputError, OutputError.MarshalJSON, OutputError.String, ValidationError.LocalizedBasicOutput
  rust    evaluate, Evaluation, FlagOutput, ListOutput, OutputUnit, ErrorEntry, ErrorDescription
  python  -
  note    the "flag" output is `is_valid` and the "basic" one `to_output`, as 2020-12 section 12 writes them

capability schema/output-hierarchical  the standard output nested as the schema is: detailed and verbose
  heroes  pending
  go      ValidationError.DetailedOutput, ValidationError.LocalizedDetailedOutput
  rust    HierarchicalOutput, OutputUnitChildren
  python  -
  note    the "detailed" and "verbose" outputs nest each error under its keyword location; not done in this round, nothing written badly in their place

capability schema/annotations  the annotations a valid instance collects: titles, defaults, formats, the members evaluated
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    Evaluation, AnnotationEntry, output::Annotations
  python  -
  note    the validator collects the annotations `unevaluatedItems` and `unevaluatedProperties` read, and gives none to the program: titles, defaults and formats collected for a valid instance are not done in this round

capability schema/drafts  the dialects read: draft 7 and 2020-12, chosen by `$schema` or by the caller
  heroes  json/schema.Draft, json/schema.Options, json/schema.DRAFT_7_URI, json/schema.DRAFT_2020_12_URI
  go      Draft, Draft.String, Draft7, Draft2020
  rust    Draft, draft7, draft202012
  python  Draft7Validator, Draft202012Validator, validators.SPECIFICATIONS, Validator.ID_OF
  note    a schema's `$schema` chooses, at a document's root and at an embedded resource; the caller chooses for one with none. The JSON Schema Test Suite, commit e2a68a15: draft 7's 1,655 required instances, 1,651 as the suite answers, and 2020-12's 2,082, 2,078 as it answers; the four of each answered otherwise are `idn-hostname`'s, below

capability schema/older-drafts  the dialects before draft 7, and 2019-09
  heroes  refused: the dialects the author named for this package are 2020-12 and draft 7 (2026-11-17); draft 4's and 6's keywords and 2019-09's `$recursiveRef` are the older forms of theirs, and a schema naming one is told `unsupported`
  go      Draft4, Draft6, Draft2019, UnsupportedDraftError
  rust    draft4, draft6, draft201909, draft4::new, draft4::options, draft4::is_valid, draft4::validate, draft6::new, draft6::options, draft6::is_valid, draft6::validate, draft201909::new, draft201909::options, draft201909::is_valid, draft201909::validate
  python  Draft3Validator, Draft4Validator, Draft6Validator, Draft201909Validator
  note    the suite's two optional cross-draft cases that reference 2019-09 are refused for that reason

capability schema/dialect-of  which dialect a schema declares
  heroes  json/schema.draft_of
  go      -
  rust    Draft (detected from `$schema`), meta::validator_for
  python  validators.validator_for
  note    a meta-schema of the program's own is followed to its dialect by the compilation, through its own `$schema`, at most eight times

capability schema/meta  a schema checked against its meta-schema, and the meta-schemas themselves
  heroes  json/schema.schema_errors_of, json/schema.meta_schema
  go      SchemaValidationError, InvalidMetaSchemaURLError, MetaSchemaCycleError, MetaSchemaMismatchError
  rust    meta::is_valid, meta::is_valid_for, meta::validate, meta::validate_for, meta::validator_for, meta::options, meta::MetaSchemaOptions, meta::MetaValidator, draft7::meta::is_valid, draft7::meta::validate, draft7::meta::validator, draft202012::meta::is_valid, draft202012::meta::validate, draft202012::meta::validator
  python  Validator.check_schema, Validator.META_SCHEMA, SchemaError
  note    the meta-schemas of the two dialects and 2020-12's seven vocabularies are carried, as json-schema.org served them on 2026-11-17, and read only where a schema or a reference names them; a meta-schema of the program's own is given as a `Resource`

capability schema/resources  the other documents a `$ref` names, given by the program
  heroes  json/schema.Resource, json/schema.Options
  go      Compiler.AddResource, ResourceExistsError
  rust    Registry, RegistryBuilder, Resource, Uri, uri::from_str, uri::resolve_against, uri::Path, uri::EncodedBuffer, uri::EncodedString
  python  referencing.Registry, referencing.Resource, Validator.resolver
  note    a document is found at the URI it is given at and at its own `$id`; a URI two documents claim is `invalid`, as Go's `DuplicateIDError`

capability schema/references  `$ref`, `$dynamicRef`, `$id`, `$anchor` and `$dynamicAnchor` resolved, and a reference that names nothing told at once
  heroes  json/schema.compile_with
  go      AnchorNotFoundError, JSONPointerNotFoundError, DuplicateAnchorError, DuplicateIDError, InvalidJsonPointerError, ParseAnchorError, ParseIDError, ParseURLError
  rust    ReferencingError
  python  referencing.exceptions.Unresolvable
  note    `$ref` by pointer, by anchor and to another document; `$dynamicRef` to the outermost `$dynamicAnchor` of its name in the dynamic scope, where its first target has one; draft 7's `$id` with a fragment as an anchor, and its `$ref` hiding its siblings. A reference into an unknown keyword is followed. A reference that comes back to a schema already running on the same place in the instance is an error, where Python raises `RecursionError` (observed on `{"$ref": "#"}`)

capability schema/fetching  a document a `$ref` names fetched from a file or the network
  heroes  refused: nothing is fetched: a schema is read by a program, and a document a reference names is read or fetched by the program, which gives it as a `Resource`; a validator that opens files or the network by what an instance's schema says is a door a schema from outside could open
  go      URLLoader, FileLoader, FileLoader.Load, FileLoader.ToFile, SchemeURLLoader, SchemeURLLoader.Load, Compiler.UseLoader, LoadURLError, UnsupportedURLSchemeError, UnmarshalJSON
  rust    HttpRetriever, AsyncHttpRetriever, HttpOptions, Retrieve, AsyncRetrieve, HttpRetrieverError, async_options, async_options_for, async_validator_for, async_validator_map_for
  python  referencing.Registry(retrieve=)
  note    -

capability schema/subschema  a schema that a URI and a fragment name inside the documents given, validated against alone
  heroes  json/schema.compile_at
  go      Compiler.Compile of "file.json#/$defs/x"
  rust    validator_map_for, ValidatorMap
  python  -
  note    the URI may name a resource and a fragment, a pointer or an anchor's name

capability schema/formats  `format` asserted where the caller asks, for every format the two dialects name
  heroes  json/schema.Options, json/schema.matches_format
  go      Compiler.AssertFormat
  rust    ValidationOptions::should_validate_formats, EmailOptions
  python  FormatChecker, FormatChecker.check, FormatChecker.conforms, FormatChecker.checkers, FormatError, Validator.FORMAT_CHECKER, Validator.format_checker
  note    every format of the two dialects, each by the grammar of its RFC. A host name's A-labels are held to IDNA2008: Punycode read back to itself, every code point PVALID by RFC 5892's derivation (tables made from Unicode 17.0.0's database), its contextual rules and RFC 5893's Bidi rule. `idn-hostname` reads a name as IDNA2008 (RFC 5891) does, with no mapping of UTS 46 first: the suite's four instances that expect a fullwidth digit, a zero width space or a fullwidth ACE prefix mapped are answered otherwise, in each dialect, and the suite's tests say so; a label is not held to NFC. Python checks nine formats with no other library installed (observed: `FormatChecker.checkers`)

capability schema/custom-formats  a format of the program's own, checked by its function
  heroes  json/schema.Format, json/schema.Options
  go      Format, Compiler.RegisterFormat
  rust    ValidationOptions::with_format, ValidationOptions::should_ignore_unknown_formats
  python  FormatChecker.checks, FormatChecker.cls_checks
  note    a format of the program's own is a name and a function, checked before the dialects' own

capability schema/content  `contentEncoding`, `contentMediaType` and `contentSchema` asserted where the caller asks
  heroes  json/schema.Options
  go      Compiler.AssertContent, Compiler.RegisterContentEncoding, Compiler.RegisterContentMediaType, Decoder, MediaType
  rust    ValidationOptions::with_content_media_type, ValidationOptions::with_content_encoding
  python  -
  note    asserted where the caller asks, as Go's `AssertContent`: the encoding `base64` and the media type `application/json`; other encodings and media types are annotations

capability schema/vocabularies  the `$vocabulary` of a meta-schema honoured: a keyword of a vocabulary it leaves out is not asserted
  heroes  json/schema.compile_with
  go      Compiler.AssertVocabs, UnsupportedVocabularyError
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a meta-schema's `$vocabulary` is read: a vocabulary it leaves out is not asserted, and one it requires and this package does not know is `unsupported`; the format-assertion vocabulary asserts `format`

capability schema/regex  the regular expressions of `pattern` and `patternProperties`
  heroes  json/schema.compile_with
  go      Compiler.UseRegexpEngine, RegexpEngine, Regexp, InvalidRegexError
  rust    Regex, FancyRegex, PatternOptions, PatternCache
  python  -
  note    a pattern is ECMA-262's, translated for `regex`, which matches in time linear in the text: `\\uXXXX`, ECMAScript's `\\s` and `.`, Unicode categories by their long names; a look-around or a back-reference is refused `unsupported` when the schema is compiled. The suite's optional ECMAScript cases pass

capability schema/custom-keywords  a keyword of the program's own, checked by its function
  heroes  pending
  go      Compiler.RegisterVocabulary, Vocabulary, SchemaExt, CompilerContext, CompilerContext.Enqueue, CompilerContext.EnqueueRef, ValidatorContext, ValidatorContext.AddErr, ValidatorContext.AddError, ValidatorContext.AddErrors, ValidatorContext.Duplicates, ValidatorContext.Equals, ValidatorContext.EvaluatedItem, ValidatorContext.EvaluatedProp, ValidatorContext.Validate, ValidatorContext.ValueLocation, DynamicRef, Enum
  rust    Keyword, ValidationOptions::with_keyword, ValidationContext
  python  validators.create, validators.extend, validators.validates, Validator.VALIDATORS, Validator.descend
  note    a keyword of the program's own, called with its value and the instance, could be a function value as a custom format is; not done in this round

capability schema/types  the types a schema names, and a type of the program's own
  heroes  refused: the types are JSON's six and `integer`, which the dialects fix; a type of the program's own is a schema the program writes
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  TypeChecker, TypeChecker.is_type, TypeChecker.redefine, TypeChecker.redefine_many, TypeChecker.remove, Validator.TYPE_CHECKER, Validator.is_type, exceptions.UnknownType, exceptions.UndefinedTypeCheck
  note    -

capability schema/bundle  a schema and every document it references made one document
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    bundle, dereference, async_bundle, async_dereference
  python  -
  note    every document a schema references written into its `$defs`; not done in this round

capability schema/canonical  a schema rewritten into a canonical form and its satisfiability asked
  heroes  refused: a schema rewritten into a canonical form and its satisfiability asked is a schema compiler's analysis, not validation
  go      -
  rust    canonicalize, canonical::options, canonical::CanonicalSchema, canonical::CanonicalizeOptions, canonical::PreparedDocument, canonical::CanonicalKind, canonical::CanonicalView, canonical::CanonicalizationError, canonical::Containment, canonical::Distinctness, canonical::ObjectViolationView, canonical::OperandMismatch, canonical::RawReason, canonical::Satisfiability, canonical::UnsatisfiableReason, canonical::Cause, canonical::ArrayView, canonical::ContainsView, canonical::IntegerView, canonical::NumberView, canonical::ObjectView, canonical::RawView, canonical::StringView, canonical::TypedGroupView, canonical::json::to_string, canonical::json::Error
  python  -
  note    -

dropped
  rust    json::cmp::equal, json::cmp::equal_arrays, json::cmp::equal_objects, json::unique::is_unique: JSON's equality, which is the language's `==` on `json.Value`
  rust    json::Json, json::Node, json::Array, json::Object, json::JsonNumber, json::SerdeJson, json::Jsonb, json::JsonbNode, json::jsonb, json::inspect, json::NodeIdentity, json::PendingError, json::stock_enum_value, json::conformance::assert_conformance, json::conformance::document: the traits that let the crate read several JSON libraries' values; here there is one, `json.Value`
  rust    json::Magnus, json::MagnusPendingErrorScope, json::RbNode, json::Pyo3, json::magnus_child, json::magnus_invalidate_members_cache, json::magnus_is_object, json::magnus_probe_root, json::magnus_take_pending_error, meta::magnus, meta::pyo3: the crate's bindings to Ruby and to Python
  rust    ValidationOptions (the builder's own plumbing past the blocks above): a field of `json/schema.Options` each
  python  Validator.evolve, validators.define, validators.field, validators.fields: the class machinery of attrs behind the validators
  python  jsonschema.protocols, jsonschema.warnings, validators.warn, validators.HashTrieMap, validators.lru_cache, validators.urldefrag, validators.urljoin, validators.urlsplit, validators.unquote, validators.deque, validators.methodcaller, validators.contextlib, validators.reprlib, validators.json, validators.Iterable, validators.Mapping, validators.Sequence, validators.TYPE_CHECKING, validators.annotations, exceptions.dedent, exceptions.defaultdict, exceptions.deque, exceptions.define, exceptions.heapq, exceptions.indent, exceptions.pformat, exceptions.re, exceptions.warnings, exceptions.Any, exceptions.ClassVar, exceptions.TYPE_CHECKING, exceptions.annotations: names other modules lend the package, no part of its API
  go      LocalizableError, ValidationError.LocalizedBasicOutput, ValidationError.LocalizedDetailedOutput, ValidationError.LocalizedError, ValidationError.LocalizedGoString: messages translated through golang.org/x/text; the messages here are English, as every message of Rebel is
